denote the set of all such graphs. Clearly, if $f(G)$ denotes the number of cycles of a graph G, then $M(k)=\\mathrm{max}\\{f(G)\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}|\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}G\\in {\\Gamma }_{k}\\}$.\n\nThroughout the paper we will tacitly assume that for each graph G in {\\Gamma }_{k}${\\Gamma }_{k}$ one Hamiltonian cycle ${C}_{G}:{u}_{1}{u}_{2}\\dots {u}_{n}$ has been fixed and we call the additional edges in $E(G)⧹E({C}_{G})$ chords. Two chords ${u}_{1}{v}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}{v}_{2}$ of some $G\\in {\\Gamma }_{k}$ are called skew, if their endvertices appear in the cyclic order {u}_{1}${u}_{1}$, {u}_{2}${u}_{2}$, {v}_{1}${v}_{1}$, {v}_{2}${v}_{2}$ on {C}_{G}${C}_{G}$. Two chords are parallel, if they are not skew.\n\nShi's main results in [3] concerning $M(k)$ are that for $k⩾1$(1)${2}^{k}+k(k-1)+1⩽M(k)⩽{2}^{k+1}-1$and that for $1⩽k⩽4$ equality holds in the left inequality of (1).\n\nWe will first prove some properties of the extremal graphs, i.e. those graphs G in {\\Gamma }_{k}${\\Gamma }_{k}$ for which $f(G)=M(k)$. As our main results we improve (1) and determine $M(k)$ and the structure of the extremal graphs exactly for $5⩽k⩽10$.\n\nOur research was initially motivated by the hope of finding some regularity in the structure of the extremal graphs which would have allowed to determine $M(k)$ exactly for all (or several) k. Unfortunately, we were unable to do so and have to leave it as a challenging open problem. The interested reader may find similar results in [2] or [4] and the references mentioned therein.\n\n## 2. Results on $M(k)$ for general k\n\nWe need some more notation and observations already used by Shi. Let $G\\in {\\Gamma }_{k}$ and let $S\\subseteq E(G)⧹E({C}_{G})$. The number of cycles C of G such that $S=E(C)⧹E({C}_{G})$ is denoted by ${g}_{G}(S)$. Therefore, $f(C)={\\sum }_{S\\subseteq E(G)⧹E({C}_{G})}\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}g(S)$. Shi observed that ${g}_{G}(S)⩽2$ for all $S\\subseteq E(G)⧹E({C}_{G})$ (Theorem 3.1 in [3]).\n\nThe next lemma describes some properties of extremal graphs.\n\nLemma 1\n\n(i) If $G\\in {\\Gamma }_{k}$ and $f(G)=M(k)$, then no two chords are incident with the same vertex, i.e. the maximum degree $\\Delta (G)$ of G is at most 3.\n\n(ii) If $G\\in {\\Gamma }_{k}$ with $f(G)=M(k)$, then there is no set of chords $S\\subseteq E(G)⧹E({C}_{G})$ such that $\\varnothing \\ne S\\ne E(G)⧹E({C}_{G})$ and each chord in S is parallel to each chord not in S.\n\n(iii) For $k⩾2$ there is a 3-regular graph $G\\in {\\Gamma }_{k}$ with $f(G)=M(k)$.\n\n(iv) For $k⩾2$ every graph $H\\in {\\Gamma }_{k}$ with $f(H)=M(k)$ arises from a 3-regular graph $G\\in {\\Gamma }_{k}$ with $f(G)=M(k)$ by replacing the edges of {C}_{G}${C}_{G}$ by non-trivial paths.\n\nProof\n\n(i) Assume to the contrary that there are chords ${u}_{1}v,{u}_{2}v\\in E(G)⧹E({C}_{G})$. Let ${N}_{G}(v)\\cap V({C}_{G})=\\{{v}^{-},{v}^{+}\\}$ and let the graph ${G}^{\\prime }\\in {\\Gamma }_{k}$ arise from G by replacing v$v$ by a path ${v}_{1}{v}_{2}$ on two vertices such that ${N}_{{G}^{\\prime }}({v}_{1})\\cap V({C}_{G})=\\{{v}^{-}\\}$, ${N}_{{G}^{\\prime }}({v}_{2})\\cap V({C}_{G})=\\{{v}^{+}\\}$ and replacing ${u}_{1}v,{u}_{2}v$ by ${u}_{1}{v}_{1},{u}_{2}{v}_{2}$ such that ${u}_{1}{v}_{2}$ and ${u}_{2}{v}_{2}$ are skew (this can be ensured by appropriately renaming of ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}$).\n\nClearly, to every cycle of G corresponds a unique cycle of {G}^{\\prime }${G}^{\\prime }$. Furthermore, we have ${g}_{G}(\\{{u}_{1}v,{u}_{2}v\\})=1<2={g}_{{G}^{\\prime }}(\\{{u}_{1}{v}_{2},{u}_{2}{v}_{2}\\})$ which implies the contradiction $f({G}^{\\prime })>f(G)$.\n\n(ii) Assume to the contrary that such a set $S=\\{{u}_{1}{v}_{1},{u}_{2}{v}_{2},\\dots ,{u}_{l}{v}_{l}\\}$ exists.\n\nWe may assume that $\\mathit{uv}\\in E(G)⧹(E({C}_{G})\\cup S)$ is such that u and {u}_{1}${u}_{1}$ partition {C}_{G}${C}_{G}$ into two paths {P}_{1}${P}_{1}$ and {P}_{2}${P}_{2}$ such that all interior vertices of {P}_{1}${P}_{1}$ have degree 2. Let {G}^{\\prime }${G}^{\\prime }$ arise from G by replacing \\mathit{uv}$\\mathit{uv}$ and ${u}_{1}{v}_{1}$ by {\\mathit{uv}}_{1}${\\mathit{uv}}_{1}$ and ${u}_{1}v$.\n\nClearly, to every cycle of G that does not contain both of \\mathit{uv}$\\mathit{uv}$ and ${u}_{1}{v}_{1}$ corresponds a unique cycle of {G}^{\\prime }${G}^{\\prime }$. By the properties of S, every cycle of G that contains \\mathit{uv}$\\mathit{uv}$ and ${u}_{1}{v}_{1}$ must go along {P}_{1}${P}_{1}$. This implies that also to every cycle of G that contains both of \\mathit{uv}$\\mathit{uv}$ and ${u}_{1}{v}_{1}$ corresponds a unique cycle of {G}^{\\prime }${G}^{\\prime }$. Finally, ${g}_{G}(\\{\\mathit{uv},{u}_{1}{v}_{1}\\})=1$ and ${g}_{{G}^{\\prime }}(\\{{\\mathit{uv}}_{1},{u}_{1}v\\})=2$ which implies the contradiction $f({G}^{\\prime })>f(G)$.\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nWe thank Colin Hirsch for his help with the computer program. Furthermore, we thank the referees for their useful hints.\n\n## References\n\n  1. [1]\n\nR. Diestel, Graph theory, Graduate Texts in Mathematics, vol. 173, Springer, Berlin, 2000, 313p.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  2. [2]\n\nR. Entringer, P.J. Slater\n\nOn the maximum number of cycles in a graph\n\nArs Combin., 11 (1981), pp. 289-294\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  3. [3]\n\nY. Shi\n\nThe number of cycles in a Hamilton graph\n\nDiscrete Math., 133 (1994), pp. 249-257\n\nView PDFView articleView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  4. [4]\n\nL. Volkmann\n\nEstimations for the number of cycles in a graph\n\nPeriod. Math. Hungar., 33 (1996), pp. 153-161\n\nView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  5. [5]\n\nH.P. Yap, S.K. Teo, On uniquely r-pancyclic graphs, in: K.M. Koh, H.P. Yap (Eds.), Graph Theory, Lecture Notes in Mathematics, vol. 1073, Springer, Berlin, 1984, pp. 334–335.\n\nGoogle Scholar--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSome Problems on Paths and Cycles (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392767794_Some_Problems_on_Paths_and_Cycles)\n