t integer as m∗(2n) rather than m(2n). A bipancyclic graph with 2n vertices and m∗(2n) edges is called minimal. Recall that if a graph G has e(G) edges and v(G) vertices, then the difference e(G) − v(G) is called the excess of G, so m∗(2n) is the value such that m∗(2n) − 2n is the minimum excess for a bipancyclic graph on 2n vertices.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA path variant of the explorer-director game on graphs (https://combinatorialpress.com/ars-articles/volume-166/a-path-variant-of-the-explorer-director-game-on-graphs/)\nciteturn13search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; In [7] Kao proved that every hypercube \\(Q_n\\) for \\(n \\ge 2\\) is bipanpositionable. In fact, Shih et. al. in [12] showed that hypercubes satisfy an even stronger property and are bipanpositionable bipancyclic.\n\n### 3. Path variant on bipanpositionable graphs\n\nThe notion of pancyclic graphs, graphs containing every possible cycle as a subgraph, was first introduced in 1971 by Bondy and has been widely studied since [2]. Here we focus on a related definition for bipartite graphs. As bipartite graphs, by definition, do not contain odd cycles they cannot be pancyclic. However, a related notion of bipancyclicity was defined by Mitchem and Schmeichel in 1982 [8].\n\nDefinition 3.1. [8] We say a graph \\(G\\) is bipancyclical if \\(G\\) contains every even cycle of length \\(4\\) to \\(|V(G)|\\) as a subgraph.\n\nLater, in 2006, Kao introduced the related notions of panpositionable and bipanpositionable graphs [7]. We will be focused only on bipartite graphs and thus only give the definition for bipanpositionable graphs below.\n\nDefinition 3.2. [7] A hamiltonian bipartite graph \\(G\\) is bipanpositionable if for any two distinct vertices \\(x,y \\in V(G)\\) and for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(dist_G(x,y) \\le k \\le \\frac{|V(G)|}{2}\\) and \\((k-d_G(x,y))\\) even, there exists a hamiltonian cycle \\(C\\) of \\(G\\) such that \\(dist_C(x,y)=k\\).\n\nIn [7] Kao proved that every hypercube \\(Q_n\\) for \\(n \\ge 2\\) is bipanpositionable. In fact, Shih et. al. in [12] showed that hypercubes satisfy an even stronger property and are bipanpositionable bipancyclic. However, as we do not require this stronger property for Theorem 3.3 we omit its definition here.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nON THE CYCLE STRUCTURE OF HAMILTONIAN *k*-REGULAR (https://www.math.uwo.ca/faculty/adamus/adamus_publications/regular_bipartite.pdf)\nciteturn13search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; It should be noted that Tian and Zang [3] proved that a hamiltonian bipartite graph of order 2*n* ≥ 120 and minimal degree greater than 2*n*/5 + 2 is necessarily bipancyclic.\nOne immediately verifies that the size of such a graph is precisely *n*²/2, so the above theorem does not apply. Instead, we prove the following result.\n\n**Theorem 2.** *If* *G* *is a hamiltonian* *n*/2*-regular bipartite graph of order* 2*n* > 8, *then* *G* *contains a cycle* *C* *of length* 2*n* − 2. *Moreover, if* *C* *can be chosen to omit a pair of adjacent vertices, then* *G* *is bipancyclic.*\n\nOur motivation for presenting Theorem 2 is that, although concerning a narrow class of graphs, it plays an important role in the general study of long cycles in balanced bipartite graphs [1]. We find it also quite amusing that the proof below relies entirely on the combinatorics of the adjacency matrix.\n\nIt should be noted that Tian and Zang [3] proved that a hamiltonian bipartite graph of order 2*n* ≥ 120 and minimal degree greater than 2*n*/5 + 2 is necessarily bipancyclic. This result leaves open the case of |*G*| = *n* < 60, in which the above theorem seems to be best to date.\n\n*Proof.* Suppose to the contrary that there is a hamiltonian *n*/2-regular bipartite graph *G* on 2*n* vertices (*n* ≥ 5), without a cycle of length 2*n* − 2. Let *X* = {*x*₁, …, *x*